Compute awk's remainder operator with fmod(), which is exact. The old
formula, x - (long long)(x / y) * y, rounds both the quotient and the
product to doubles, so once x exceeds 2^53 in magnitude the remainder
is wrong: "1e17 % 7" gave 0 instead of 5. Beyond the range of long long
the conversion is undefined, and an infinite y gave NaN for any finite
x rather than x.

---
 editors/awk.c |    2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/editors/awk.c
+++ b/editors/awk.c
@@ -3946,7 +3946,7 @@
 			case '%':
 				if (R_d == 0)
 					syntax_error(EMSG_DIV_BY_ZERO);
-				L_d -= (long long)(L_d / R_d) * R_d;
+				L_d = fmod(L_d, R_d);
 				break;
 			}
 			debug_printf_eval("BINARY/REPLACE result:%f\n", L_d);
